}]\nFirstly, we perform a normal Verlet update and then apply constraints. \
Since each bond affects both vertices, the \"information\" (momentum, \
tension, etc.) travels through all nodes of the system. This allows, for \
instance, if our *mesh* hits the ground with one of its vertices, the whole \
body will stop and bounce back.\n\nIn addition, I will give an example \
similar to what you have seen in the video:\n\n### Image to \
Mesh\n*Transformation of the b/w image to a mesh of interconnected \
vertices*\n\n:::tip\nGo to the header section to download this notebook\n:::"\
, "Display" -> "codemirror", "Hash" -> 
     "6e812dcb-2a7c-4126-9d7d-593503ea595e", "Invisible" -> False, 
    "MetaOnly" -> False, "Props" -> <|"Hidden" -> True|>, 
    "PublicFields" -> {"Properties"}, "State" -> "Idle", "Type" -> "Input", 
    "UID" -> Null, "Notebook" -> "b1000fb1-c9cc-423c-925a-13fafc2266f2"|>, 
   <|"Data" -> "## Constraint Algorithm\n\nWhenever you try to model a system \
with multiple hard or soft bonds connecting different nodes, it quickly \
becomes a pain. This is one of those cases where solving the problem using \
only positions\[LongDash]without momentum and forces-becomes a game \
changer.\n\nAs mentioned in [my previous \
post](https://wljs.io/blog/2025/05/01/rim):\n\n> By directly adjusting the \
positions of two points connected by a bond to maintain the bond length, we \
effectively account for all forces in a single step.  \n> The stiffness of \
the bond is not directly part of the differential equation\[LongDash]it's \
introduced through how you enforce the constraint: either strictly (hard) or \
partially (soft).\n\n\n<div class=\"text-center w-full\"><svg version=\"1.1\" \
